{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '176,245p' ../datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/index.cljc; sed -n '945,970p' ../datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/main.cljc; rg -n 'would-block-object|readtable-copy' ~/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/lib/_io#.scm; ls -la host/gambit/yin\"","aggregated_output":"\n\n(defn make-indexer\n  \"The indexer for one session: `observer`, attached to `program-out`\n   beside the evaluator's; `:local`, the complete-retention log its\n   transactions are committed to (dao.space.transactor requires memory-log\n   retention); and `content-store`, the `dao.jing` byte store publications\n   materialize into.  `session-token` is the shell's stable token, recorded\n   as provenance.\n\n   `publication-policy` is the composition's immutable generic publication\n   policy (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md section 5):\n   `{:branching-factor n :max-payload-bytes b}`, where a nil byte ceiling\n   means no declared ceiling.  It is validated here, at assembly, so a\n   policy storage would refuse is refused before any round publishes with\n   it, and it reaches every publication path the indexer takes — the bulk\n   reconstruction, the incremental suffix fold, and the `index/restore`\n   between them — so all of them see the same numbers.  The map is retained\n   verbatim under `:publish-opts`, which `transactor/publish!` takes as-is:\n   it may also carry the caller's `:select-stream`, which is intake\n   selection and not policy.  `:publish-opts` is the older spelling of the\n   same input and is still accepted; `publication-policy` wins.\n\n   `publication-media` is the composition's durable-publication medium\n   (`yin.repl.publication`): the request side a round appends its\n   `{:op :publish ...}` to and the result side it reads the matching\n   `{:op :publication-result ...}` from.  Only those two sides and the\n   result cursor are retained here — a `:local` durable HEAD writer on the\n   supplied medium is the composition's to keep and service\n   (`yin.repl.publication/service-local!`), never the indexer's to call —\n   so the indexer holds no callback.  Nil, a store that declares no durable\n   HEAD, settles every round locally at once.\n\n   `:allocator` retains only the transactor authority, with its intake\n   detached. `:materialization` retains verified coverage as plain data:\n   `{:cursor c :transactions n :manifest-address a :manifest m :max-t t\n   :policy p}`, `p` being the policy that publication actually cut at — the\n   declared byte ceiling beside the manifest's own count cap, which a\n   bounded bulk build may have lowered. `:publication-seq` is the session's\n   publication-request counter, so every `:id` it emits is unique within the\n   session; `:pending-publications` is the serialized queue of the requests\n   no result has settled yet, oldest first, each `{:request r :promotes\n   frontier}`.  A round's fresh `:publication` intake, pool and\n   tree builder are dropped at its end; no earlier publication payload is\n   retained.\"\n  [{:keys [observer session-token content-store publish-opts publication-media]\n    :as spec}]\n  (let [opts (or (:publication-policy spec) publish-opts)\n        _ (index/publication-policy opts)]\n    {:observer observer\n     :local (memory-log)\n     :content-store content-store\n     :session-token session-token\n     :publish-opts opts\n     :publication-media (some-> publication-media publication/engine-media)\n     :publication-seq 0\n     :pending-publications []\n     :next-e datom/first-user-id\n     :transactions 0\n     :published 0\n     :published-payloads 0\n     :manifest-address nil\n     :allocator nil\n     :materialization nil\n     :lost? false\n     :failure nil}))\n\n\n(defn publication-policy\n  \"The indexer's immutable generic publication policy\n\n(defn request-stop!\n  \"The explicit stop trigger: append the shell's own quit line to the input\n   medium.\n\n   This is the whole of what a host signal handler, a headless supervisor, or a\n   test does to stop the composition.  It is a line producer like any other, so\n   the one step owner still performs the shutdown: `(quit)` stops the shell,\n   and the shell stopping is what stops the endpoint.  The wake source, when\n   one is supplied, is nudged so the step owner runs the quit line at once\n   rather than at the idle interval it had armed.\"\n  ([state]\n   (driver/submit-line! (:input state) \"(quit)\"))\n  ([state w]\n   (let [result (request-stop! state)]\n     (when w (wake/nudge! w))\n     result)))\n\n\n(defn stop-tick\n  \"One shutdown tick for a storage composition's transport state and the\n   served endpoint, both of which have already been asked to stop\n   (`composition-stop`, `serve/stop!`).  Returns `[state server lines\n   stopped?]`, the composition's lines before the endpoint's.\n\n   `serve/stopped?` is the whole of the endpoint's exit condition, and the\ntotal 0\ndrwxr-xr-x@  5 sto  staff   160 Nov 20 01:03 .\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 43 sto  staff  1376 Nov 20 01:24 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x@  6 sto  staff   192 Nov 20 02:20 dao\ndrwxr-xr-x@  3 sto  staff    96 Nov 20 01:03 yang\ndrwxr-xr-x@  3 sto  staff    96 Nov 20 01:03 yin\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
